From f5f44cb0175ad2767b0152c0e1fc52bc15630330 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MaiRat <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 10:20:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Raise the missing early SyntaxErrors for var/lexical
 conflicts, labelled-function loop bodies, and script exports

Three families of parse-time early error were not being raised, so the
engine accepted (or, in one case, crashed on) code the specification
rejects at compile time.

VarDeclaredNames and LexicallyDeclaredNames of a scope must be disjoint.
The parser tracked var/let collisions only order-dependently and lost the
information once a var hoisted out of the block it was written in, so
`var x; let x;`, `let x; { var x; }`, `for (let x of []) { var x; }`,
`try {} catch ([e]) { var e; }` and their siblings were all silently
accepted, the later declaration winning. Each scope now records its own
VarDeclaredNames as a var hoists through it, and both a var hoisting into a
lexical binding and a lexical declared where a var has hoisted through are
rejected -- at every scope level, in either declaration order, and across a
for-head, switch or destructured catch parameter. A block-nested function
declaration is lexical for this purpose, so it conflicts with a same-named
var while two block functions of one name still coexist in sloppy mode.

A labelled function declaration is never a legal loop body. It was caught
for a non-lexical head, but a let/const for-in/for-of/C-style head rewrites
its body into a synthetic per-iteration block, which hid the labelled
statement from validation. The loop parser now rejects a labelled-function
body on the statement as written, before that rewrite.

An `export` in script code is an early error -- its bindings target the
host-injected module `exports` object, which a plain script does not have.
`export default <expr>` dereferenced the absent binding and surfaced a
NullReferenceException; every export form in a script now raises a clean
SyntaxError instead.

Track1LanguageTests covers all three families, including guards that valid
var/lexical combinations in different scopes, a var deduping against a
parameter, a labelled function at statement position, and duplicate sloppy
block functions are all still accepted.
